17] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b483f9f7-47a9-5725-b1f1-fe0ea39a7e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb0ae539-5348-5a2c-b226-c009ce6d6094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1045 "Divisibility of Integers by 3, 9, 6, and 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d1fb6020-a735-5706-80b8-bc04b0d8b879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $66\\,333\\,9◻0$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c81002d7-9f3e-57b8-8938-1545e4037d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172311 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n6+6+3+3+3+9+◻+0=30+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,900,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+0=30,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,930,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+3=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,990,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+9=39,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1061 172668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1794b655-2bac-5203-8fdb-02da477fb69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1061 172669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b30e2ba3-941c-5d16-b861-02db07423b35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1061 172672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c70f43-c735-5619-a8c7-49afb6e904cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1061 172673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46310e8f-1f56-5e58-9836-71090df92551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1061 172676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c02867b-43a4-5116-88da-bc7736bfb7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1061 172677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "777acec7-1e70-587a-80c0-746fd1cdb9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we studied some rules for determining if an integer is divisible by $2,5,10,4,$ and $8.$ In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of divisibility rules to include $3,9,6,$ and $7.$\n\nWe have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n312\\,561\n$$\n\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18.\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3,$ we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n*A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$*\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1065 9390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b0f6b89-66c8-5e63-9825-60eb62f1271f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1064 "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n*To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ If we double this digit, we get $2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} =  {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ Then, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42.\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7,$ we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1065 9391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5cdfa6e-09a2-5600-9083-a424aaf3d959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6,$ we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}.$ Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- We now check for divisibility by $3.$ The sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30.\n$$\n\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3,$ we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3,$ we conclude that it is divisible by $6.$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1065 9441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "955bbb9e-e7e0-552d-9ecf-ef5efd79239a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482798 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482798 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482798 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482798 #edb